<.shell current={:wallet_badges}>
  <h1>Wallet badges</h1>
  <p class="lead">Apple's and Google's own Add to Wallet badges.</p>

  <div class="demo">
    <.wallet_badges id="wallet-badges-demo" apple_href="#" google_href="#" />
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    On an ID card's page, <code>/cards/:token</code>, the one page where a member adds their card. Its email links there, and so do Your membership and the Dashboard's to-do, with a link that says Add ID card to Wallet. One page to learn, and on a computer it has the QR code to open it on the phone.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <p>
    Anywhere else. Another page links to the card's page instead, and a page for team or site admins, such as Adminland's Integrations test, uses a button that says what it tests: a pass there would land on the admin's phone.
  </p>

  <h2>Brand rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Both are the brands' own files, unchanged: Apple's English badge and Google's condensed Canadian English button. Never redraw, recolour, or crop them, and never put them in a <code>&lt;.button&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The same height, 48px, Google's minimum. Google's condensed button is two lines like Apple's, so the pair fits on one line on a 375px phone. Apple has no wider badge for the web.
    </li>
    <li>No other button on the page is bigger than them, as Google asks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.wallet_badges id="…" apple_href=&#123;…&#125; google_href=&#123;…&#125; /&gt;</code>. Each href is a route that builds the pass when tapped. A badge shows only when its Wallet is set up.
  </p>
</.shell>
